00:21:45,570 --> 00:21:48,369
And you've got to satisfy the

520
00:21:48,369 --> 00:21:51,169
habitual, skillful, literate readers in the center

521
00:21:51,169 --> 00:21:54,579
at the same time as satisfying the people

522
00:21:54,579 --> 00:21:57,990
on the outskirts that are not habitual readers.

523
00:21:58,770 --> 00:22:01,320
And so that is a skill

524
00:22:01,320 --> 00:22:03,870
in itself to make a multi-level proposition.

525
00:22:03,870 --> 00:22:06,510
And how does that play out? Like what is a...

526
00:22:07,590 --> 00:22:08,270
How do you actually...

527
00:22:08,310 --> 00:22:10,890
How do you think about structurally doing that?

528
00:22:10,890 --> 00:22:13,890
I think structurally what you've got to

529
00:22:13,890 --> 00:22:16,890
do is you've got to have a style

530
00:22:17,610 --> 00:22:21,510
that is both palatable and somewhat enjoyable

531
00:22:21,510 --> 00:22:25,410
to the habitual readers in the center,

532
00:22:25,410 --> 00:22:28,440
but that is also useful to the

533
00:22:28,440 --> 00:22:31,470
people on the outside. And that's inevitably,

534
00:22:31,470 --> 00:22:34,500
that style needs to be therefore propulsive. I do a

535
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:37,530
lot of it instinctively, but I spend a lot of time

536
00:22:38,310 --> 00:22:42,530
concentrating on rhythm, the rhythm of a sentence.

537
00:22:42,530 --> 00:22:44,700
Because a book is however

538
00:22:44,700 --> 00:22:46,870
many thousand sentences in a row,

539
00:22:46,870 --> 00:22:48,710
and you've got to make it

540
00:22:48,710 --> 00:22:50,550
so that each sentence has a rhythm,

541
00:22:50,550 --> 00:22:52,859
and that rhythm must always

542
00:22:52,859 --> 00:22:55,169
be tripping forward, forward, forward.

543
00:22:55,169 --> 00:22:57,929
So this rhythm is less artistic and much

544
00:22:57,929 --> 00:23:00,690
more about that word that you came back to,

545
00:23:00,690 --> 00:23:02,500
propulsion, like the rhythm of

546
00:23:02,500 --> 00:23:04,310
movement and progress and pace.

547
00:23:04,310 --> 00:23:04,890
Absolutely.

548
00:23:04,890 --> 00:23:05,490
Momentum.

549
00:23:05,490 --> 00:23:07,590
It's about propulsion. It's as if...

550
00:23:08,310 --> 00:23:12,590
It's as if I'm creating a style that the

551
00:23:12,590 --> 00:23:16,870
experienced reader in the center appreciates as a style.

552
00:23:16,870 --> 00:23:19,269
You know, call it whatever you want, faux naïve

553
00:23:19,269 --> 00:23:21,669
or this or that, they'll find a word for it.

554
00:23:21,669 --> 00:23:24,669
But the people on the outskirts, the non-habitual readers,

555
00:23:24,669 --> 00:23:27,669
it's like I have my hand gently on their

556
00:23:27,669 --> 00:23:31,229
back just pushing them through. They don't notice.

557
00:23:31,229 --> 00:23:34,790
They're not aware of it, but I am

558
00:23:34,790 --> 00:23:36,950
just easing them through the process.

559
00:23:37,429 --> 00:23:37,929
And...

560
00:23:38,310 --> 00:23:41,830
You know, one of the most heartfelt compliments

561
00:23:41,830 --> 00:23:45,350
that you get, I've had dozens, hundreds of people

562
00:23:45,350 --> 00:23:49,830
say to me, oh, I loved your book. I finished

563
00:23:49,830 --> 00:23:54,310
it. It is such an achievement for some, you know,

564
00:23:55,030 --> 00:23:58,190
not habitual readers, some unfamiliar, unaccustomed readers

565
00:23:58,190 --> 00:24:01,350
to finish a book. It's a sense of

566
00:24:01,350 --> 00:24:04,230
achievement, self-esteem, and so on. And you do

567
00:24:04,230 --> 00:24:07,110
that by helping them finish the book. You push

568
00:24:07,110 --> 00:24:07,700
them gradually through. You push them through the

569
00:24:07,700 --> 00:24:08,290
process. You push them through the process. You

570
00:24:08,310 --> 00:24:08,320
let them breathe. You push them through the

571
00:24:08,320 --> 00:24:08,330
process. You push them through the process. They

572
00:24:08,330 --> 00:24:11,110
don't notice, but you're driving them through it. And

573
00:24:11,110 --> 00:24:13,890
you do that by propulsion, and you do the

574
00:24:13,890 --> 00:24:17,110
propulsion by sentence structure so that the

575
00:24:17,110 --> 00:24:20,330
beat is always tipping forward, forward, forward. It's

576
00:24:20,330 --> 00:24:22,210
like a great pop song. If you look at

577
00:24:22,210 --> 00:24:24,090
a great pop song back in the days before,

578
00:24:25,810 --> 00:24:28,950
before, you know, all this electronic stuff came

579
00:24:28,950 --> 00:24:32,090
along, the rhythm will speed up through the song

580
00:24:32,090 --> 00:24:35,120
very, very, very subtly. Look at a Beatles

581
00:24:35,120 --> 00:24:38,150
song, especially live. You know, Ringo will be driving,

582
00:24:38,150 --> 00:24:41,270
in that drive in that tempo and it always

583
00:24:41,270 --> 00:24:44,390
ends up a little faster than it begins. Nobody

584
00:24:44,390 --> 00:24:47,039
notices but it's there and that's what you got

585
00:24:47,039 --> 00:24:49,689
to do on the page. You've got to you got

586
00:24:49,689 --> 00:24:52,499
to make it so that it's a little bit

587
00:24:52,499 --> 00:24:55,310
like a like a carnival ride you know where you

588
00:24:55,310 --> 00:24:58,190
it's they're slipping down this polished tube you

589
00:24:58,190 --> 00:25:01,070
know and there's no getting out of it

590
00:25:01,070 --> 00:25:03,249
once they're in it they can't get out.

591
00:25:03,249 --> 00:25:05,429
And now tell me more about that sense of

592
00:25:05,430 --> 00:25:07,440
propulsion because there you're talking about sentences

593
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:09,450
but then there's the sense of propulsion

594
00:25:09,450 --> 00:25:11,250
of opening a question at the beginning keeping

595
00:25:11,250 --> 00:25:13,050
that question open answering at the end. There's

596
00:25:13,050 --> 00:25:14,360
the sense of propulsion that comes at the end

597
00:25:14,360 --> 00:25:15,670
of a chapter of hey we're going to have

598
00:25:15,670 --> 00:25:18,020
a cliffhanger. Television does this and in

599
00:25:18,020 --> 00:25:20,370
the next episode you're going to learn something

600
00:25:20,370 --> 00:25:23,430
like that. Yeah well that's and that's really where

601
00:25:23,430 --> 00:25:26,490
I got it from I did 40,000 hours of

602
00:25:26,490 --> 00:25:29,820
television of every kind and it kind of bakes

603
00:25:29,820 --> 00:25:33,150
it into your DNA. And ending a chapter to

604
00:25:33,150 --> 00:25:34,430
me is never thought about.

605
00:25:34,430 --> 00:25:35,430
It is always

606
00:25:35,430 --> 00:25:38,030
utterly sort of instinctively obvious. When you

607
00:25:38,030 --> 00:25:40,630
say that do you mean that the book

608
00:25:40,630 --> 00:25:42,370
begins to reveal itself that hey this is

609
00:25:42,370 --> 00:25:44,110
where it should be or is it something else.

610
00:25:44,110 --> 00:25:46,800
Yeah exactly you and even the end of a

611
00:25:46,800 --> 00:25:49,490
book I remember one of my books the hard way

612
00:25:49,490 --> 00:25:52,720
I think it was it was that book where

613
00:25:52,720 --> 00:25:55,950
I remember finishing a chapter very late on and thinking

614
00:25:55,950 --> 00:25:57,860
great you know I'm almost done I've just got

615
00:25:57,860 --> 00:25:59,770
to kind of do I don't know maybe a chapter

616
00:25:59,770 --> 00:26:02,550
and a half to wrap it up. And then I

617
00:26:02,550 --> 00:26:05,330
suddenly realized no this is the end of the book. This

618
00:26:05,330 --> 00:26:07,910
is done now.

619
00:26:07,910 --> 00:26:13,440
Because you just know. It's all about asking

620
00:26:13,440 --> 00:26:18,970
the question and TV had a thing there was

621
00:26:18,970 --> 00:26:23,230
a situation in 1980 that changed completely

622
00:26:23,230 --> 00:26:27,490
by 1990 by 1990 people had something that

623
00:26:27,490 --> 00:26:30,270
they did not have in 1990

624
00:26:30,270 --> 00:26:33,050
and it utterly changed the business.

625
00:26:33,050 --> 00:26:33,249
And you see now you are thinking on television. At

626
00:26:33,249 --> 00:26:33,449
leastiyorsun wasn't the robot you see on television, was it.

627
00:26:33,449 --> 00:26:34,330
It was. lack of건

628
00:26:34,330 --> 00:26:36,110
Now you are thinking, why?

629
00:26:36,310 --> 00:26:37,189
What is he talking about?

630
00:26:37,530 --> 00:26:39,239
What did they not have in

631
00:26:39,239 --> 00:26:40,949
1980 that they did have in 1990?

632
00:26:41,230 --> 00:26:44,169
I've implied a question there.

633
00:26:44,810 --> 00:26:47,230
And you now want the answer.

634
00:26:47,230 --> 00:26:51,510
And the answer is remote control.

635
00:26:52,650 --> 00:26:55,400
In 1980, people actually physically had to get

636
00:26:55,400 --> 00:26:58,150
up off the sofa and change the channel.

637
00:26:58,669 --> 00:26:59,929
In 1990, they could do it

638
00:26:59,929 --> 00:27:01,189
with the push of a button.

639
00:27:01,530 --> 00:27:03,189
And that utterly changed the business.

640
00:27:03,190 --> 00:27:05,390
And because you could rely on

641
00:27:05,390 --> 00:27:07,590
a certain amount of laziness in 1980,

642
00:27:07,750 --> 00:27:09,810
you could rely that if they finish one program,

643
00:27:09,910 --> 00:27:11,290
they're going to stick around through

644
00:27:11,290 --> 00:27:12,670
the commercial break for the next one.

645
00:27:13,470 --> 00:27:16,110
By 1990, absolutely, all bets were off.

646
00:27:16,110 --> 00:27:19,090
They could jump around at the press of a finger.

647
00:27:19,730 --> 00:27:21,010
And that utterly changed it.

648
00:27:21,090 --> 00:27:23,210
And so how did we react to that?

649
00:27:23,570 --> 00:27:25,780
We reacted to it by

650
00:27:25,780 --> 00:27:27,990
something that has largely disappeared now,

651
00:27:28,050 --> 00:27:29,950
but you still see, for instance,

652
00:27:29,950 --> 00:27:31,850
in baseball or certain sports where,

653
00:27:32,530 --> 00:27:33,170
you know, in baseball,

654
00:27:33,190 --> 00:27:35,440
you get to the sort of fourth inning and it's

655
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:37,690
pretty clear maybe which way the game's going and so on.

656
00:27:37,730 --> 00:27:39,850
And so they usually have a trivia question

657
00:27:39,850 --> 00:27:42,670
that they'll tell you the answer at the end

658
00:27:42,670 --> 00:27:45,490
of the break when they return to the game.

659
00:27:46,210 --> 00:27:49,220
And it's incredibly powerful that humans are

660
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:52,230
hardwired to want to know the answer,

661
00:27:52,590 --> 00:27:54,910
even if they're not interested in the subject.

662
00:27:55,710 --> 00:27:57,010
You know, if it's a movie

663
00:27:57,010 --> 00:27:58,310
show or something, you might say,

664
00:27:58,630 --> 00:28:01,750
who was the first choice for Dirty Harry?

665
00:28:01,750 --> 00:28:06,030
In terms of casting, we'll tell you after the break.

666
00:28:06,370 --> 00:28:08,710
And people are like, whoa, who was the first choice?

667
00:28:08,790 --> 00:28:10,350
Wasn't Clint Eastwood the first choice?

668
00:28:10,570 --> 00:28:11,790
And they want to know.

669
00:28:12,010 --> 00:28:14,610
Even if they do know, they want to

670
00:28:14,610 --> 00:28:17,210
stick around for the gratification of being proved right.

671
00:28:17,330 --> 00:28:18,370
That's why people love Jeopardy.

672
00:28:18,870 --> 00:28:19,230
Yeah.

673
00:28:19,730 --> 00:28:21,290
And, you know, the answer to who

674
00:28:21,290 --> 00:28:22,850
was first choice, believe it or not,

675
00:28:22,890 --> 00:28:24,750
for Dirty Harry was Frank Sinatra.

676
00:28:25,730 --> 00:28:27,510
He turned down the part.

677
00:28:28,070 --> 00:28:31,730
So we learned that humans are hardwired to want,

678
00:28:31,810 --> 00:28:33,010
the answer to a question.

679
00:28:33,550 --> 00:28:36,000
And I think, actually, that is

680
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:38,450
the easiest part of constructing a novel.

681
00:28:40,130 --> 00:28:41,390
Absolutely the easiest part.

682
00:28:41,470 --> 00:28:42,530
You imply a question.

683
00:28:42,630 --> 00:28:43,830
It doesn't matter what it is.

684
00:28:44,030 --> 00:28:45,570
It doesn't matter how important it is.

685
00:28:46,150 --> 00:28:48,470
People will stick around to find out the answer.

686
00:28:49,410 --> 00:28:52,410
And so that really, plotting in that sense,

687
00:28:52,910 --> 00:28:56,590
is way overestimated in terms of difficulty.

688
00:28:56,590 --> 00:28:58,530
Plotting is really pretty easy.

689
00:29:00,270 --> 00:29:01,590
And now, as a writer,

690
00:29:01,750 --> 00:29:05,230
do you feel like you're going on an adventure yourself,

691
00:29:05,370 --> 00:29:09,710
kind of like fingers are following the story or something?

692
00:29:09,970 --> 00:29:11,130
Yeah, I mean, absolutely.

693
00:29:11,950 --> 00:29:13,430
Again, I try and replicate.

694
00:29:13,730 --> 00:29:15,490
What I want is this feeling

695
00:29:15,490 --> 00:29:17,250
that I have as a reader.

696
00:29:17,810 --> 00:29:18,610
And you know this.

697
00:29:18,750 --> 00:29:21,850
When you've got a really great book on the go,

698
00:29:22,770 --> 00:29:25,390
you're really angry if you have to put it down.

699
00:29:25,550 --> 00:29:28,210
And you just cannot wait to get back to it.

700
00:29:28,210 --> 00:29:29,930
You know, let's say you've got to

701
00:29:29,930 --> 00:29:31,650
do a chore or an errand or something.

702
00:29:31,750 --> 00:29:32,550
Or go to work.

703
00:29:32,970 --> 00:29:34,050
And then you get back and you

704
00:29:34,050 --> 00:29:35,130
pick up the book and you're like,

705
00:29:35,190 --> 00:29:36,750
ah, what's going to happen next?

706
00:29:36,810 --> 00:29:37,110
Yeah.

707
00:29:37,250 --> 00:29:38,870
I need that feeling as a writer.

708
00:29:39,130 --> 00:29:40,790
It's incredibly strong as a reader.

709
00:29:40,930 --> 00:29:42,530
I remember one Christmas day,

710
00:29:43,130 --> 00:29:47,270
I was, our daughter was working in a cinema.

711
00:29:47,850 --> 00:29:51,030
And she was doing the day shift on Christmas Day.

712
00:29:51,710 --> 00:29:54,100
And therefore, our Christmas was going to

713
00:29:54,100 --> 00:29:56,490
start when she finished work and got back,

714
00:29:56,490 --> 00:29:58,590
which was maybe, I don't know, six o'clock or something.

715
00:29:59,210 --> 00:30:00,910
So I was reading a book.

716
00:30:01,750 --> 00:30:03,610
That afternoon, it was a great book.

717
00:30:03,610 --> 00:30:04,810
I was just loving it.

718
00:30:04,810 --> 00:30:07,060
And I was thinking, I was hoping

719
00:30:07,060 --> 00:30:09,310
that there would be a big snowstorm

720
00:30:09,310 --> 00:30:11,730
and she couldn't get there or something like that.

721
00:30:11,730 --> 00:30:13,570
I was hoping that she wouldn't arrive so

722
00:30:13,570 --> 00:30:15,410
that I could keep on reading the book.

723
00:30:15,410 --> 00:30:17,550
And that's the power of story.

724
00:30:17,550 --> 00:30:20,380
And I want to feel that power when

725
00:30:20,380 --> 00:30:23,210
I'm writing it as well as reading it.

726
00:30:23,590 --> 00:30:25,660
Well, you were talking about needing

727
00:30:25,660 --> 00:30:27,730
to know the answer to the question,

728
00:30:27,730 --> 00:30:29,030
kind of being pulled along.

729
00:30:29,570 --> 00:30:31,650
The other word that came to mind for me,

730
00:30:31,650 --> 00:30:33,410
it was just the feeling of being absorbed.

731
00:30:33,410 --> 00:30:35,120
Absorbed in another world, absorbed

732
00:30:35,120 --> 00:30:36,830
in a pattern of language,

733
00:30:36,830 --> 00:30:40,030
absorbed in characters and scenes.

734
00:30:40,350 --> 00:30:41,989
Absolutely immersed, I call it.

735
00:30:41,989 --> 00:30:43,570
And that is such a subtle thing.

736
00:30:43,570 --> 00:30:44,870
I mean, how do you do it?

737
00:30:45,749 --> 00:30:46,669
Nobody knows.

738
00:30:46,669 --> 00:30:47,229
No, but I do.

739
00:30:47,229 --> 00:30:49,929
It absolutely happens or it doesn't happen.

740
00:30:50,469 --> 00:30:51,749
You know, that's one of the strange things,

741
00:30:51,749 --> 00:30:54,479
there's all kinds of cliches, you know,

742
00:30:54,479 --> 00:30:57,209
or jokes about how to write a book.

743
00:30:57,209 --> 00:30:59,039
You know, people say a

744
00:30:59,039 --> 00:31:00,870
thriller, it needs just two things.

745
00:31:01,650 --> 00:31:03,709
Unfortunately, nobody knows what they are.

746
00:31:03,709 --> 00:31:06,890
And that's kind of true.

747
00:31:06,890 --> 00:31:08,069
I was so excited for you

748
00:31:08,069 --> 00:31:09,249
to tell me what they were.

749
00:31:09,249 --> 00:31:10,189
Exactly.

750
00:31:10,189 --> 00:31:11,890
You know, it's a mystery.

751
00:31:11,890 --> 00:31:16,590
Sometimes it grabs you and sometimes it doesn't.

752
00:31:16,590 --> 00:31:18,410
Is that individual to the reader?

753
00:31:18,410 --> 00:31:19,769
Is it general?

754
00:31:19,769 --> 00:31:21,659
Can you trust it for a

755
00:31:21,659 --> 00:31:23,550
large population as opposed to individual readers?

756
00:31:24,290 --> 00:31:25,489
I don't know.

757
00:31:25,489 --> 00:31:27,309
I think certain books appeal

758
00:31:27,309 --> 00:31:29,130
to different people in different ways.

759
00:31:29,989 --> 00:31:30,610
But yeah.

760
00:31:30,610 --> 00:31:31,489
Either you are.

761
00:31:31,490 --> 00:31:32,930
I think there's a lot of people

762
00:31:32,930 --> 00:31:34,370
who are immediately sucked into the world.

763
00:31:34,370 --> 00:31:36,530
And I think probably the way to

764
00:31:36,530 --> 00:31:38,690
do that is not try too hard.

765
00:31:38,690 --> 00:31:40,640
I think that is absolutely true

766
00:31:40,640 --> 00:31:42,590
of practically every aspect of writing.

767
00:31:42,590 --> 00:31:44,130
Don't try too hard.

768
00:31:44,130 --> 00:31:51,650
There's a great, great quote by David Mamet, a screenwriter.

769
00:31:51,650 --> 00:31:55,340
He was talking about actors in this particular quote,

770
00:31:55,340 --> 00:31:59,030
but really it can be about book characters or anything.

771
00:31:59,030 --> 00:32:00,030
And he said,

772
00:32:00,030 --> 00:32:00,630
essentially,

773
00:32:00,630 --> 00:32:02,909
if you're an actor who steps onto the

774
00:32:02,909 --> 00:32:05,189
stage or steps onto the screen, and he says,

775
00:32:05,189 --> 00:32:08,229
hey, I'm the main character.

776
00:32:08,229 --> 00:32:12,650
And the audience says, are we going to like you?

777
00:32:12,650 --> 00:32:15,090
And the worst possible answer

778
00:32:15,090 --> 00:32:17,530
is to say, yes, you are.

779
00:32:17,530 --> 00:32:19,429
And I'll tell you why.

780
00:32:19,429 --> 00:32:20,689
That's the worst possible answer.

781
00:32:20,689 --> 00:32:22,610
The best possible answer.

782
00:32:22,610 --> 00:32:24,810
Are we going to like you?

